Open evidence tasks

01 — Widespread or rapid lake warming

Need for

Introduction;

Requirements


supports for the statement that lake warming is widespread or rapid;
better have multiple, and with actual data;
at least 5 papers;


Evidences


Warming but variation, O’Reilly et al. (2015): 235 lakes, 1985–2009, 0.34°C decade-1, range -0.7–1.3°C decade-1.





02 — Warming is not spatially consistent

Need for

Introduction;

Requirements


coexisting warming and cooling, or strongly contrasting trends within the nearby regions
quantitative statement, e.g. metrics of spatial auto-correlation, like Moran’s I blabla. Evidences










03 — A single long-term slope leaves temporal pathways unresolved

Need for

Introduction, paragraphs 2–3; Discussion, paragraph 1.

Requirements


record usual per-lake temporal summaries in global LSWT studies;
identify whether any comparator tests spatially organised, multidecadal annual-rate pathways;
search principal global comparators and at least one review with documented inclusion criteria;
write a bounded gap statement, never “first” or “never studied”.


Evidences








04 — Earlier temporal-pattern work and distance from this question

Need for

Introduction, paragraph 3.

Requirements


compare one regional annual regime-shift study with present object;
compare one global seasonal-curve or functional-temperature study;
record response variable, temporal axis, spatial domain, and outcome for each;
state what transfers and what does not;
do not use seasonal thermal types as evidence for multidecadal annual warming pathways.


Evidences

Open evidence tasks

01 — Why LSWT is a consequential climate-response variable

Need for

Introduction, paragraph 1.

Requirements


support links from LSWT change to ice cover, stratification, oxygen, or thermal habitat;
retain lake-type, depth, and observation conditions;
support exact stated consequence only;
do not extend surface temperature to whole-water-column response without evidence.


Evidences








02 — Established controls on lake surface warming

Need for

Introduction, paragraph 1; Discussion, section 3.

Requirements


support atmospheric forcing, surface energy exchange, ice state, and morphology as context;
retain seasonal and lake-type conditions;
include review and empirical sources;
do not collapse controls into universal explanation.


Evidences








03 — Warm-season and cold-season asymmetry

Need for

Results, section 2; Discussion, section 3.

Requirements


support effects of frozen states, ice phenology, and seasonal surface-energy conditions;
find source distinguishing ice-covered observations from liquid-water response;
support warm-season interpretation boundary;
reject additive seasonal attribution and ice-day co-variation as ice-feedback proof.


Evidences








04 — Teleconnections as context for regional lake-temperature variability

Need for

Introduction, paragraph 4; Discussion, section 3.

Requirements


identify lake-specific and broader climate-context evidence;
retain season, region, lag, and mechanism-evidence conditions;
compare prior designs with present global correlation fields;
reject universal NAO/AO pathway claim.


Evidences








05 — Mechanism evidence required for attribution

Need for

Discussion, sections 3–4.

Requirements


identify variables, comparison designs, and assumptions needed for attribution;
name missing energy-balance and circulation evidence;
express future work as testable design, not generic request for more data.


Evidences

Outputs and decision gate
Step 14 produces lake-by-endpoint rate/state files and a per-lake alignment summary. The first outputs are:

multi-series trajectories for representative lakes and spatially aggregated dynamics;
maps of seasonal, maximum/minimum-temperature, and ice-state alignment;
continuous distributions of endpoint-rate and ice-state change.


Step 14 输出逐湖逐终点 rate/state 文件和对齐摘要。首轮产物是代表湖多序列轨迹、空间汇总动态图、季节/极值/冰态对齐地图，以及 endpoint 变化的连续分布。

Open evidence tasks

01 — GLAST is a reconstructed surface-temperature product

Need for

Introduction, paragraph 5; Methods, Data and scope; Discussion, Limitations.

Requirements


identify observations, model forcing, calibration, coverage, and uncertainty;
obtain a dataset-description sentence and a limitation sentence from GLAST paper;
avoid calling GLAST direct in-situ observation.


Evidences








02 — Theil–Sen slopes and serial dependence

Need for

Methods, Primary trajectory metrics.

Requirements


support estimator definition;
identify serial-dependence limits on trend significance;
support descriptive-slope wording, not naive Mann–Kendall inference.


Evidences








03 — Endpoint-aligned rolling slopes as local-rate descriptors

Need for

Methods, Primary trajectory metrics; Results, section 1.

Requirements


find environmental precedent for rolling robust slopes;
support endpoint alignment;
support local-rate wording;
reject instantaneous physical-acceleration wording.


Evidences








04 — STL as PCA-only low-frequency preprocessing

Need for

Methods, Spatially balanced trajectory decomposition.

Requirements


support STL decomposition and trend-window meaning;
find one relevant environmental preprocessing use;
support PCA-only use while raw annual LSWT remains primary;
avoid claiming physically true trend or complete noise removal.


Evidences








05 — PCA/EOF of multidecadal trajectories

Need for

Methods, Spatially balanced trajectory decomposition; Results, section 3.

Requirements


support PCA/EOF as covariance description for trajectories;
find general method source and environmental application;
support boundary: variance-maximising modes are not independent mechanisms.


Evidences








06 — Retaining and matching secondary PCs

Need for

Results, section 3; Discussion, section 2.

Requirements


support interpretation of close eigenvalues, sign, rank exchange, and matching;
map evidence to actual LOCO matching procedure;
support PC2–PC3 joint structure and lower prominence of PC4–PC5;
avoid stable-component equals stable-mechanism claim.


Evidences








07 — Equal-area aggregation changes spatial estimand

Need for

Introduction, paragraph 4; Methods, Spatially balanced trajectory decomposition; Discussion, section 2.

Requirements


support effects of unequal sampling density and spatial autocorrelation;
define equal-area occupied-cell estimand;
state it is not a full spatial covariance model;
read spatial-sampling source and spatially indexed PCA or functional-data source.


Evidences








08 — Spatial and temporal hold-outs

Need for

Methods, Stability and external association; Results, sections 3–4.

Requirements


support why random record-wise splits fail under spatial or temporal dependence;
identify failures addressed by spatial blocks, LOCO, and leave-one-decade-out;
state remaining validation limits.


Evidences








09 — Teleconnection fields, lag selection, and causality

Need for

Introduction, paragraph 4; Methods, Stability and external association; Results, section 4; Discussion, sections 3–4.

Requirements


support limits from serial dependence, lag screening, spatial multiplicity, and unmeasured pathways;
support NAO/AO as reproducible spatial association, not causal pathway;
read autocorrelated-correlation, field-significance or multiplicity, and causal-boundary sources;
reject lag-as-travel-time or forcing-direction claim.


Evidences








10 — Why SFPCA is informative but not active method

Need for

Methods or supplementary method rationale, only if needed.

Requirements


identify SFPCA assumptions on regularity, completeness, and stationarity;
compare them with global occupied-lake-cell data;
distinguish equal-area balance from explicit spatial-dependence modelling;
read Kuenzer, Hörmann & Kokoszka (2021) and one supporting spatial-data source;
keep out of manuscript unless reviewer or comparison requires it.


Evidences

The active descriptive metrics now use raw annual mean LSWT:

long-term warming: Theil–Sen slope of raw annual mean LSWT, reported as °C per 40 years;
annual warming speed: adjacent-year raw annual LSWT difference;
acceleration: Sen slope of annual warming speed over 1982–2020.

STL is retained only to construct a low-frequency trajectory before PCA, with nt=99. PCA interprets PC1–PC5 (82.1% cumulative variance); 95% cumulative variance is a diagnostic, not a retention rule.
STARS / ST_AIS and Step 12 are retired from the active workflow. They must not re-enter the draft through historical helpers or output directories.

